This depends upon 

1) How the nodes are configured

A VMS cluster consisting of upto 96 nodes could pretty well be managed by the 
same number as look after a single node.

2) How often things are changed on the nodes.

If you need to upgrade two clusters at the same time - then generally you
will need at least two people :)

If all your systems are running legacy applications and are updated once in a
blue moon - then you need fewer people.

3) Whether the system managers are looking after other things on the systems

   eg 

   Acting as oracle DBAs
   Dealing with Mail problems
   Developing Applications etc

4) Whether you have any ancillary staff - operators etc who can run procedures 
   written by the system managers to add users, load and check backup tapes etc

5) If you are not running VMS but are running Unix or WNT then in addition to
   these considerations you need to add :-

   Regularly patching the operating system
   Regularly monitoring for Hacking attempts 
   (you should do this on VMS as well but on WNT and Unix it is more likely
    the attempt was successful and you will need to take action to block the
    hole or restore the operating system).
    

I currently look after

6 VMS 'clusters' (some of these are single systems but as I said a cluster is 
as easy to manage as a single system).
+ a few VMS workstations.

My team consists of myself and two others looking after these VMS systems.

These systems are fairly active with new software, printer queues etc being
installed/setup regularly. 
The team also does some development work, looks after mail, acts as Oracle DBAs
on one system etc.
We create procedures to allow our operations department to perform standard
functions such as adding users and performing backups.
We work with people who look after/develop various administrative applications
running on a number of these systems.

I also spend time together with an additional team member looking after the
few Academic Unix systems together with our Unix DNS and Usenet newserver.
The Unix side has always been understaffed here.


David Webb
VMS and Unix team leader
CCSS
Middlesex University   



